<!-- ==================== 1. ABSTRACT AND KEYWORDS ==================== -->
<h2>1. ABSTRACT AND KEYWORDS</h2>
<div class="box-section">
  <p><strong>Abstract:</strong> Modern higher education students experience persistent cognitive fatigue and elevated rates of deadline omission caused by unstructured, cross-channel academic announcements broadcast across messaging groups, email, and fragmented institutional portals. Conventional learning management systems function strictly as passive repositories devoid of semantic reasoning, temporal grounding, or cognitive workload synthesis.</p>
  <p>In this work, we present <strong>AcademicAI</strong>, a production-grade, multi-tier academic intelligence and personalized student assistance platform that converts raw academic notices into verified deliverables and structured faculty evaluation workflows. The computational architecture incorporates statistical natural language processing, regex-grounded entity extraction, temporal normalization to Asia/Kolkata (IST), an automated 5-way notice relationship classifier, an invariant additive priority model, and an AI-assisted rubric evaluation pipeline with deterministic offline fallback.</p>
  <p>Evaluated on an authentic benchmark of 110 curated academic notices (50 TRAIN, 25 DEV, 35 TEST), the system achieved <strong>76.7% multi-class intent categorization accuracy</strong> (71.1% macro-F1) across 11 notice classes, <strong>93.0% exact-match named entity extraction</strong>, <strong>94.1% temporal normalization accuracy</strong>, <strong>100% additive priority score invariance</strong>, and <strong>100% confirmation gating for destructive state mutations</strong>. Optical character recognition on degraded scans yielded 3.19% CER and 18.94% WER. The dual-mode evaluation pipeline demonstrated verified criterion-grounded grading (82.5/100 on capstone engineering submissions). The platform is deployed as a high-performance Next.js 15 application supporting student workload management and faculty course evaluation.</p>
  <p style="margin-top: 8pt;"><strong>Keywords:</strong> Academic Natural Language Processing, Information Extraction, Temporal Normalization, Cognitive Workload Intelligence, Rubric Evaluation, Human-in-the-Loop AI.</p>
</div>

<!-- ==================== 2. INTRODUCTION ==================== -->
<h2>2. INTRODUCTION</h2>
<p>Modern university engineering curricula demand concurrent engagement across 5 to 7 multidisciplinary courses. At Vellore Institute of Technology (VIT), each course encompasses continuous assessment tests (CAT-1, CAT-2), laboratory exercises, project reviews (EPJ), and final assessment tests (FAT). Because faculty communicate assignment releases, timetable alterations, and quiz announcements through informal circulars, WhatsApp class groups, Canvas, and VTOP, students process 25 to 35 unstructured messages weekly. Empirical research shows this decentralization causes a 34% deadline oversight rate.</p>
<p><strong>Problem Statement:</strong> Unstructured academic notices lack standardized schema representations. When an instructor posts: <em>"The CSE4001 project review originally scheduled for this Wednesday in SJT 402 is postponed to next Monday at 2:00 PM on Canvas; late submissions will incur a 5-mark penalty"</em>, standard tools fail to: (1) semantically parse the cancellation while generating the rescheduled deadline, (2) normalize relative dates ("next Monday at 2:00 PM") to an absolute timestamp in Asia/Kolkata, and (3) recalculate the student's cognitive workload to prevent assessment collisions.</p>
<p><strong>Measurable Objectives:</strong></p>
<ol>
  <li>Attain &ge; 75% accuracy and macro-F1 on multi-class intent classification across 11 academic notice types.</li>
  <li>Achieve &ge; 90% exact-match accuracy in named entity and temporal extraction normalized to Asia/Kolkata.</li>
  <li>Develop a 100% mathematically transparent, additive priority scoring engine and cognitive workload partitioner that excludes completed and cancelled tasks.</li>
  <li>Construct an authorized faculty portal featuring course-specific rubric management and AI-assisted PDF evaluation with offline deterministic fallback.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Key Contributions:</strong> (1) An end-to-end ingestion pipeline handling raw text, PDF, and scanned image circulars; (2) An explainable 6-factor additive priority scoring model (0–100); (3) Multi-horizon cognitive workload partitioning across Today, Tomorrow, 7 Days, and Beyond; and (4) A dual-engine submission evaluation system pairing Google Gemini 2.5 Flash with a robust offline deterministic fallback.</p>

<!-- ==================== 3. LITERATURE REVIEW ==================== -->
<h2>3. LITERATURE REVIEW</h2>
<p>We review relevant literature across three computational paradigms: (1) Classical & Rule-Based Academic Extraction (Chowdhury et al., 2021; Zhang et al., 2022); (2) Transformer Encoders in Educational NLP (Devlin et al., 2019; Sanh et al., 2020 DistilBERT; Beltagy et al., 2020 SciBERT; Liu et al., 2021); and (3) Temporal Reasoning & Workload Intelligence (Vijk et al., 2023; Sharma & Patel, 2024; Kumar et al., 2024; Latif et al., 2025).</p>

